  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe employ a gradient-based optimization with local search, starting from the supplied initial_h_values. The core idea is to iteratively reduce the worst-case overlap integral by focusing on the lag with the maximum current overlap. This heuristic reduces the problem to a series of manageable local optimization steps, each targeting a specific lag. This approach balances computational efficiency with the ability to find a better solution than the initial configuration. The limitations include potential local minima and sensitivity to the initial configuration, but we mitigate this by using a sufficiently large number of iterations and adaptive gradient steps.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h as a vector of size n_points, with constraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nObjective: Minimize\nC\u2085 = max_k [ (\u2211_{x=0}^{2 - k} h[x] * (1 - h[x + k]) ) * dx ]\n\nConstraints are enforced via Lagrange multipliers during gradient steps and by clamping values to [0, 1], then projecting back to the correct sum after each update. The derivative for each lag k is computed as:\ndf/dh[m] = dx * [ (1 - h[m + k]) * \u03c7(m \u2264 n_points - k - 1) - h[m - k] * \u03c7(m \u2265 k) ]\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStart with the initial_h_values. Initialize the best solution as the initial configuration. For each iteration:\na. Compute the full correlation for all lags using np.correlate\nb. Identify the lag k with the maximum overlap value\nc. Compute the gradient for lag k\nd. Update h using gradient descent: h_new = h - learning_rate * gradient\ne. Clamp values to [0, 1]\nf. Ensure \u2211h = n_points / 2 by scaling h = h * (target_sum / \u2211h)\ng. Check if the maximum overlap has decreased; if not, adapt learning rate or terminate\nRepeat for a fixed number of iterations (e.g., 500) or until the overlap converges to a plateau.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Learning rate: starts at 0.1, reduces by 0.95 every 10 iterations\n- Iteration limit: 500 iterations or until overlap improves by less than 1e-4\n- Random restarts: every 100 iterations, perturb h by \u00b10.05 with 0.5 probability\n- Multiple lags: in the final 100 iterations, optimize the top 3 lags in each step\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nFor n_points=500, each iteration computes O(n\u00b2) overlaps and O(n) gradients. With 500 iterations, the total complexity is O(n\u00b3). This fits within 600s on a single CPU. To handle numerical instability:\n- Clamp h[i] to [0, 1] after gradient steps\n- Use a minimum learning rate of 0.001\n- Track the best solution seen so far and return it on termination\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nAfter optimization, validate:\n- sum(h) must equal n_points / 2\n- all h[i] in [0, 1]\n- re-calculate C\u2085 using the original correlation function\nInclude sanity checks:\n- If any h[i] exceeds [0, 1], return the initial configuration\n- If the overlap increases after 50 iterations, reduce learning rate\n- If the overlap is below target (0.3807), return the solution",
